n pages disagree on the status. A GA announcement is dated\"\nwiki/cloudflare/python-workers.md:12 | rule 27 | \"Anything built on gRPC is out\" | \"Nothing built on gRPC runs\"\nwiki/cloudflare/python-workers.md:12 | rule 29 | \"HTTP clients are routed through JS fetch.\" | \"Pyodide routes HTTP clients through JS fetch.\"\nwiki/cloudflare/vitest-plugin.md:11 | rule 36 | \"It needs a Workflow binding; one taken from ctx.exports raises an error explaining how to add one.\" | \"modify needs a Workflow binding. A Workflow taken from ctx.exports raises an error that explains how to add the binding.\"\nwiki/cloudflare/vitest-plugin.md:14 | rule 14 | \"is undocumented: the Workflow engine runs in a Durable Object\" | \"is undocumented. The Workflow engine runs in a Durable Object\"\n\u003c/supervisor-agent\u003e\n\u003c/pasted_content id=\"4850\"\u003e"}]}
{"v":1,"agent":"claude-code","cli_version":"0.12.1","type":"assistant","ts":"2026-10-05T02:30:30.581Z","id":"msg_011CfiJb7vRWy9ShumtVHYno","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":397,"content":[{"id":"toolu_01RXkezNEzEAWxAZp8mPG4LP","input":{"command":"cd /home/agus/workspace/asermax/nagara/.scratch/learn-architecture-exploration \u0026\u0026 for f in SKILL.md exploration.md iterations.md wiki/workflows-iteration.md wiki/cloudflare/containers.md wiki/cloudflare/python-workers.md wiki/cloudflare/vitest-plugin.md; do echo \"=== $f\"; cat -n \"$f\"; done","description":"Print the seven edited files with line numbers"},"name":"Bash","result":{"output":"=== SKILL.md\n     1\t---\n     2\tname: architecture-exploration\n     3\tdescription: Draws a system's architecture on a canvas and explores ways to change it with the user. It lays the options out side by side, draws a part they share step by step, and redraws the whole chosen option after each decision the user makes, until one architecture remains. The user edits the same canvas. Use when the user wants to see an architecture or compare options for changing one before a design is settled. For a settled design drawn as one page, use mahou:one-page-design.\n     4\targument-hint: \u003cthe system or the change to draw\u003e\n     5\t---\n     6\t\n     7\tLoad mahou:basics first, then mahou:canvas. Then read `.mahou/architecture-exploration.md` if present.\n     8\t\n     9\t# Architecture exploration\n    10\t\n    11\tThe document goes in `canvas/` in the change's scratch folder and is named after what it draws. Every drawing of one exploration shares one document: the system as it stands in a square of its own, then the options and the drawings that follow them.\n    12\t\n    13\t## Input\n    14\t\n    15\tDraw what the conversation has established about the system: what the scouts and researchers found, and the decisions the user has settled. When drawing shows a gap, such as a part whose place nobody has stated, ask the user about it.\n    16\t\n    17\t## A drawing\n    18\t\n    19\tDraw with the shapes, labels and layout mahou:canvas sets. On top of those:\n    20\t\n    21\t- **A part is named as the system names it**, with what it runs on and the deployable it belongs to, such as `API: \u003cruntime\u003e` or `\u003cworkflow name\u003e (\u003cdeployable\u003e/)`.\n    22\t- **One area per platform or deployable**, labelled with its name. A drawing is one square: an `area` titled after what it shows, such as `\u003cletter\u003e. \u003coption\u003e` or `Chosen: \u003coption\u003e`.\n    23\t- **A note for what the arrows cannot show**, such as where deferred work runs or what an option gives up, with its arrow to the part it is about.\n    24\t- **Every shape id starts with the drawing's key**, `\u003ckey\u003e-\u003cpart\u003e`. A whole drawing can then be moved, spread or copied by its prefix, and its arrows follow through their bindings.\n    25\t\n    26\t## Exploring\n    27\t\n    28\tFollow `references/exploration.md`.\n    29\t\n    30\t## Export\n    31\t\n    32\tExport through mahou:canvas. Save the SVG next to the document, under the same name.\n    33\t\n    34\t## What this skill is not\n    35\t\n    36\tIt does not pick an option. The options and every change to them come from the user. It does not commit.\n=== exploration.md\n     1\t# Exploring options\n     2\t\n     3\tEach step adds drawings; none removes one the user has not asked to remove.\n     4\t\n     5\t1. **Lay the options out as a grid.** When a change varies along independent dimensions, such as where the code runs and how deferred work runs, draw one square per combination: one dimension along the rows, the other along the columns, each square titled `\u003cletter\u003e. \u003cvalue\u003e + \u003cvalue\u003e`. Keep the same parts in the same places across the squares, so only what differs between them moves. Give each square a note on what that combination implies that its arrows do not show.\n     6\t\n     7\t2. **The user narrows the grid.** A dropped option stays on the canvas unless the user asks to remove it. When the user changes one value of a dimension, apply the change to every square that has that value. For example, a separate consumer asked for in one queue option goes into every queue square.\n     8\t\n     9\t3. **Draw a shared part step by step.** When several options share a part whose inside matters, such as a workflow or a pipeline, draw it once in its own square below the grid. Title it with the options it belongs to, and draw each step numbered with the stores and services it reaches.\n    10\t\n    11\t4. **Draw the chosen option whole.** In a new square, draw the option the user picks with the shared part drawn inline and the neighbouring subsystems it touches, so one drawing shows the whole system.\n    12\t\n    13\t5. **Redraw after each decision.** When a decision changes the chosen option, draw it again in a new square beside the earlier one, titled `Chosen: \u003coption\u003e`, and keep the earlier one. Leave out what the user has not asked to show yet, such as a fallback. Update the note of every drawing a decision changes.\n    14\t\n    15\tThe exploration ends when the user says which drawing is the architecture.\n=== iterations.md\n     1\t# Iterations\n     2\t\n     3\t## skills/canvas/SKILL.md: mockups without a natural width\n     4\t\n     5\tOld:\n     6\t\u003e A mockup is the content of the canvas's custom `mock` shape, for anything the stock tldraw shapes do not draw, such as a screen. Its file exports `mockup`, a `MockDefinition\u003cProps\u003e` from `@canvas/MockShape`: its natural width, its default props and a function that renders them.\n     7\t\n     8\tNew:\n     9\t\u003e A mockup is the content of the canvas's custom `mock` shape, for anything the stock tldraw shapes do not draw, such as a screen. Its file exports `mockup`, a `MockDefinition\u003cProps\u003e` from `@canvas/MockShape`: its natural width, its default props and a function that renders them. The canvas draws a mockup with no natural width, such as the built-in `area`, at the shape's own size instead of scaling it, so its border and label keep their size however large the shape is.\n    10\t\n    11\t## skills/canvas/SKILL.md: shapes, labels, layout and overlaps (added to Drawing, after \"Text is black, arrow labels included.\")\n    12\t\n    13\tNew:\n    14\t\u003e - **Shapes say what a thing is.** A rectangle is one of the system's own modules, coloured by the deployable it belongs to. A cylinder is where something is stored, titled with the product that stores it, such as `\u003cprovider\u003e Postgres`, and captioned with what it holds. A cloud is an external service. An ellipse is the client. An `area` is a platform or a deployable around its parts.\n    15\t\u003e - **An arrow's label says what flows and how it travels**: the payload or the action, and the binding, protocol or credential it goes through when that matters.\n    16\t\u003e - **Lay a flow out in reading order.** It starts on the left with whoever sets it off. Stores sit in a column of their own next to what writes them, and external services go along the far edge. A process of steps is a vertical column of numbered steps, each with the store or service it reaches on the same row, so its arrow runs straight across.\n    17\t\u003e - **Leave room under a title.** The first shape inside an area sits clear of the area's label.\n    18\t\u003e - **Nothing overlaps.** No shape, label or arrow sits on another. When a straight arrow would cross a shape or another arrow's label, curve it with `bend` or move its label with `labelPosition`. When labels crowd each other, move the shapes apart.\n    19\t\n    20\t## skills/canvas/SKILL.md: the watch\n    21\t\n    22\tOld:\n    23\t\u003e - **\"Send to Claude\" notifies that a batch of edits is ready.** The document is already saved. Keep a watch on `canvas.sh watch` while the canvas is open; each line is a click. Do not react to the user's edits between clicks. When the watch times out, start it again.\n    24\t\n    25\tNew:\n    26\t\u003e - **\"Send to Claude\" notifies that a batch of edits is ready.** The document is already saved. While the canvas is open, keep `canvas.sh watch` running under a monitor that notifies you on every line it prints; each line is a click. A background command that reports only when it exits never tells you about a click, and you never read the user's notes. Do not react to the user's edits between clicks. When the monitor expires, start it again.\n    27\t\n    28\tReason: the session ran the watch as a background command; two clicks were logged and never reached the agent until the user asked.\n    29\t\n    30\t## skills/canvas/scripts/app/src/MockShape.tsx: area at its own size\n    31\t\n    32\tOld:\n    33\t```tsx\n    34\texport interface MockDefinition\u003cP extends object = Record\u003cstring, never\u003e\u003e {\n    35\t  width: number\n    36\t```\n    37\t```tsx\n    38\t  // A labelled region around the shapes that belong together; drawn behind them, it does not move them along like a frame\n    39\t  area: { width: 800, defaults: { label: '' }, render: ({ label }) =\u003e \u003cArea label={label} /\u003e },\n    40\t```\n    41\t```tsx\n    42\t    // Content is laid out at the mockup's natural width and scaled to the shape, so a large mockup and a thumbnail share one layout\n    43\t```\n    44\t\n    45\tNew:\n    46\t```tsx\n    47\texport interface MockDefinition\u003cP extends object = Record\u003cstring, never\u003e\u003e {\n    48\t  width?: number\n    49\t```\n    50\t```tsx\n    51\t  // A labelled region around the shapes that belong together; drawn behind them, it does not move them along like a frame.\n    52\t  // It has no natural width, so its border and label keep their size at any area size\n    53\t  area: { defaults: { label: '' }, render: ({ label }) =\u003e \u003cArea label={label} /\u003e },\n    54\t```\n    55\t```tsx\n    56\t    // Content is laid out at the mockup's natural width and scaled to the shape, so a large mockup and a thumbnail share one\n    57\t    // layout; a mockup with no natural width is laid out at the shape's own size\n    58\t```\n    59\t\n    60\tReason: scaling from 800 px shrank a 300-wide area's label and border to a third and enlarged a 2,500-wide one threefold.\n    61\t\n    62\t## skills/explore/SKILL.md: two loads beside the prototype one (after step 4 of \"Each question\")\n    63\t\n    64\tNew:\n    65\t\u003e 5. When the question is what a system's architecture is, or how options for changing it compare, load mahou:architecture-exploration and answer on its canvas instead of prose. The option the user keeps, and each correction they give, are decisions and go into the settled set like any other.\n    66\t\u003e\n    67\t\u003e 6. When the question can only be answered by running code, such as whether an approach holds on the real platform, load mahou:spike and answer with what the spike found. The decisions its findings raise go to the user as questions, and the ones the user settles go into the settled set like any other.\n    68\t\n    69\t## skills/guide/SKILL.md: The usual order\n    70\t\n    71\tOld:\n    72\t\u003e A question that reading and asking cannot settle goes to mahou:spike, which answers it with throwaway code that never merges.\n    73\t\n    74\tNew:\n    75\t\u003e A question that reading and asking cannot settle goes to mahou:spike, which answers it with throwaway code that never merges. A question about what a system's architecture is, or how options for changing it compare, goes to mahou:architecture-exploration, which draws them on a canvas the user edits.\n    76\t\n    77\t## README.md: Asked for by name, after mahou:one-page-design\n    78\t\n    79\tNew:\n    80\t\u003e - `mahou:architecture-exploration`. Draws a system's architecture on a `canvas` and explores ways to change it with you. The options sit side by side as a grid of small drawings, one per combination, and a part they share is drawn step by step. After each decision it redraws the chosen one whole, beside the earlier ones, until one architecture remains. Loaded by `explore` when a question is about an architecture.\n=== wiki/workflows-iteration.md\n     1\t# skills/wiki/references/cloudflare/workflows.md\n     2\t\n     3\tOld:\n     4\t\u003e verified: 2026-09-13\n     5\t\n     6\tNew:\n     7\t\u003e verified: 2026-10-04\n     8\t\n     9\tOld:\n    10\t\u003e - Payloads: params, events and non-stream step results are each capped at 1 MiB. Larger artifacts go to external storage (R2) with a reference passed instead. Whether `run()`'s final output shares the 1 MiB cap is undocumented.\n    11\t\n    12\tNew:\n    13\t\u003e - Payloads: params, events and non-stream step results are each capped at 1 MiB. Since 2026-03-26 a JS step can return a `ReadableStream\u003cUint8Array\u003e`, which the cap does not count. Otherwise larger artifacts go to external storage (R2) with a reference passed instead. Whether `run()`'s final output shares the 1 MiB cap is undocumented.\n    14\t\n    15\tAdded at the end of Knowledge:\n    16\t\u003e - A Workflow class is written in TypeScript or JavaScript (GA since 2025-04-07) or Python (open beta since 2025-08-22, no later GA entry). Python has the same step API in decorator form (`@step.do`, `step.sleep`, `step.wait_for_event`). Rust cannot define one: workers-rs has no `WorkflowEntrypoint`. Whether stream returns and the CPU setting work from Python is undocumented.\n    17\t\u003e - A step is ordinary Worker code, so it reaches code in other languages through what it can call: a service binding to another Worker, a Container through its Durable Object, or plain HTTP. A Worker in another script starts the Workflow through a binding with `script_name`; whether that works across languages is undocumented.\n    18\t\u003e - Steps per instance: 10,000 by default on Paid, configurable to 25,000 (1,024 on Free); `step.sleep` does not count as a step. State per instance is up to 1 GB on Paid.\n    19\t\u003e - Testing: see `vitest-plugin`.\n    20\t\n    21\tAdded to Sources:\n    22\t\u003e - Python Workflows: https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/python/\n    23\t\u003e - workers-rs Workflows issue: https://github.com/cloudflare/workers-rs/issues/663\n    24\t\n    25\t# Index lines\n    26\t\n    27\tskills/wiki/references/cloudflare/INDEX.md, in alphabetical order with the existing lines:\n    28\t\u003e - [`cf-cli`](cf-cli.md): the beta `cf` CLI against wrangler for D1 migrations, deploys and CI\n    29\t\u003e - [`containers`](containers.md): running an existing image behind a Durable Object, reaching bindings, sizes, sleep and cost\n    30\t\u003e - [`d1`](d1.md): SQLite on Cloudflare, its limits, wrangler migrations, and why SQLAlchemy and Alembic do not drive it\n    31\t\u003e - [`python-workers`](python-workers.md): running a Python web app as a Worker, the Pyodide package gaps and the limits\n    32\t\u003e - [`r2`](r2.md): S3 access with boto3, presigned URLs only on the account host, and pricing\n    33\t\u003e - [`vitest-plugin`](vitest-plugin.md): testing Workflows with introspection and step mocks, and outbound HTTP after `fetchMock`\n    34\t\u003e - [`workflows`](workflows.md): running a long job in a spawn-and-resolve shape, the limits that bind it, and the languages that can write one\n    35\t\n    36\tskills/wiki/references/INDEX.md:\n    37\t\u003e - [`cloudflare`](cloudflare/INDEX.md): running jobs, gates, agents, containers and data on Cloudflare's platform\n    38\t\u003e - [`modal`](modal/INDEX.md): calling Modal functions from outside its Python client\n    39\t\n    40\tskills/wiki/references/modal/INDEX.md (new):\n    41\t\u003e # Modal\n    42\t\u003e\n    43\t\u003e - [`web-endpoints`](web-endpoints.md): spawning a function and polling its result over HTTP, without the Python client\n=== wiki/cloudflare/containers.md\n     1\t# Cloudflare Containers: reaching them and what they cost\n     2\t\n     3\tverified: 2026-10-04\n     4\t\n     5\tAnswers how an existing container image runs on Cloudflare, how traffic and bindings reach it, its sizes and sleep behaviour, and what it costs. Distilled from documentation while weighing a move of a Python API image onto Cloudflare; nothing was run.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Knowledge\n     8\t\n     9\t- Containers have been GA since 2026-04-13 and need Workers Paid. Any Linux image runs, so native libraries and gRPC clients work.\n    10\t- A container is reached only through a Durable Object, with `getContainer(env.X, id)` or `ctx.container` inside the object, so every request to it goes through a Worker and a Durable Object.\n    11\t- Bindings (D1, R2, KV, Durable Objects) do not reach the container directly. Its outbound HTTP goes through outbound handlers, programmable egress proxies mapped per host with `outboundByHost`. Code inside the container reaches D1 through its REST API or a Worker acting as a proxy, and R2 through its S3 API.\n    12\t- Instance types run from lite (1/16 vCPU, 256 MiB, 2 GB disk) through basic (1/4, 1 GiB, 4 GB), standard-1 (1/2, 4 GiB, 8 GB), standard-2 (1, 6 GiB, 12 GB) and standard-3 (2, 8 GiB, 16 GB) to standard-4 (4, 12 GiB, 20 GB). The image cannot exceed the instance disk. Each account has 50 GB of image storage.\n    13\t- Cold starts are \"often 1-3 s\". The container sleeps after `sleepAfter`, 10 min by default. Disk is ephemeral and resets on each start. Shutdown sends SIGTERM, then SIGKILL up to 15 min later.\n    14\t- Pricing is per 10 ms of active running. Workers Paid includes 25 GiB-hours of memory, 375 vCPU-minutes and 200 GB-hours of disk a month; beyond that $0.0000025 per GiB-second, $0.000020 per active vCPU-second and $0.00000007 per GB-second of disk. Egress is $0.025/GB in North America and Europe with 1 TB included. An always-on 1 GiB instance comes to about $7 a month; one that sleeps most of the time fits inside the included hours.\n    15\t\n    16\t## Sources\n    17\t\n    18\t- https://developers.cloudflare.com/containers/\n    19\t- https://developers.cloudflare.com/containers/platform-details/limits/\n    20\t- https://developers.cloudflare.com/containers/platform-details/outbound-traffic/\n    21\t- https://developers.cloudflare.com/containers/pricing/\n=== wiki/cloudflare/python-workers.md\n     1\t# Python Workers: what runs and what does not\n     2\t\n     3\tverified: 2026-10-04\n     4\t\n     5\tAnswers whether a Python web app, such as a FastAPI service, can run as a Cloudflare Python Worker, which packages it can use, and the limits that bind it. Distilled from documentation and the Pyodide package list while weighing a move of a Python API onto Cloudflare; nothing was run.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Knowledge\n     8\t\n     9\t- FastAPI runs through a built-in ASGI connector (`workers.asgi`). ASGI and WSGI support landed 2026-09-02, and Python 3.14 on Pyodide 314.x is the default for compatibility dates from 2026-09-08. Whether an older compatibility date still pins an older Python is undocumented.\n    10\t- Cloudflare's own pages disagree on the status. A GA announcement is dated 2026-09-21, while a docs page still says beta behind the `python_workers` flag, with `pywrangler` for setup and deploys.\n    11\t- Packages are pure-Python wheels, PyEmscripten wheels and what Pyodide bundles. Pyodide 314.0.7 ships Pillow, httpx, SQLAlchemy, pydantic, aiohttp, asyncpg and cffi. It does not ship cairosvg (which needs native libcairo), grpcio, boto3, botocore, greenlet, markdown-it-py or google-genai; a pure wheel may still install, but nothing confirms it.\n    12\t- Nothing built on gRPC runs, so a Python client that talks gRPC, such as Modal's, cannot run there. Pyodide routes HTTP clients through JS `fetch`.\n    13\t- Limits are the Workers ones: 128 MB memory per isolate including WASM, a 64 MiB uncompressed bundle (changed 2026-09-04), CPU 30 s by default and up to 5 min on Paid, `waitUntil` up to 30 s after the response, 15 min of wall time for queue consumers and cron triggers.\n    14\t- Cold start uses a memory snapshot taken at deploy; one third-party measurement put it near 1 s.\n    15\t- Bindings to D1, R2, KV, Durable Objects, Queues, Workflows, Workers AI and service bindings are available from Python. Async SQLAlchemy through a D1 driver is not (see `d1`).\n    16\t\n    17\t## Sources\n    18\t\n    19\t- https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/languages/python/\n    20\t- https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/languages/python/packages/fastapi/\n    21\t- https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/limits/\n    22\t- https://pyodide.org/en/stable/usage/packages-in-pyodide.html\n    23\t- https://blog.cloudflare.com/python-workers-ga\n=== wiki/cloudflare/vitest-plugin.md\n     1\t# Testing Workers and Workflows with the vitest plugin\n     2\t\n     3\tverified: 2026-10-04\n     4\t\n     5\tAnswers how Cloudflare's vitest integration tests Workflows and outbound HTTP, and what replaced its removed fetch mock. Distilled from the plugin's type declarations, changelog and examples, and the docs; nothing was run.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Knowledge\n     8\t\n     9\t- `@cloudflare/vitest-plugin` is the renamed `@cloudflare/vitest-pool-workers` (renamed in 1.0.0; the old name stopped at 0.22.0). Tests run inside workerd with the project's wrangler bindings.\n    10\t- Workflow introspection is documented and supported. `introspectWorkflowInstance(binding, id)` gives `waitForStepResult`, `waitForStatus`, the output and error, and `modify(fn)` with `disableSleeps`, `mockStepResult`, `mockStepError`, `forceStepTimeout` and `mockEvent`. `introspectWorkflow(binding)` applies the same `modify` to every instance created afterwards, for ids the test does not control. Both support `await using`.\n    11\t- `modify` works on whole steps. A mocked step never runs its callback, so the HTTP calls inside it never happen; steps that are not mocked run their real code, `fetch` included. `modify` needs a Workflow binding. A Workflow taken from `ctx.exports` raises an error that explains how to add the binding.\n    12\t- `fetchMock` from `cloudflare:test` was removed in 0.13.0 and the `miniflare.fetchMock` option in 0.20.0. The changelog points to mocking `globalThis.fetch` or MSW (\"recommended\"), and the examples include an MSW fixture set up through `setupFiles`. The prose docs mention neither.\n    13\t- Miniflare's `outboundService` replaces a Worker's whole outbound traffic with a handler; whether the plugin accepts it as an option is unstated.\n    14\t- Whether MSW or a patched `globalThis.fetch` catches a `fetch` made inside a Workflow step is undocumented. The Workflow engine runs in a Durable Object, and the examples only cover requests made from the Worker itself.\n    15\t- Code-level seams, such as a global symbol the code reads for a test double, are not a documented Workflow-testing method; they put the test hook in production code paths.\n    16\t\n    17\t## Sources\n    18\t\n    19\t- https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/testing/vitest-integration/test-apis/\n    20\t- https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/build/test-workflows/\n    21\t- https://github.com/cloudflare/workers-sdk (vitest-plugin CHANGELOG, `fixtures/vitest-plugin-examples/request-mocking`, `fixtures/vitest-plugin-examples/workflows`)","status":"success"},"type":"tool_use"}]}
